>
  <p>If the windowsill is dusty again soon after cleaning, start with the route into the room. Spring pollen and outdoor dust can enter through openings or arrive on shoes, clothes, pets and carried items.</p>
  <p>The goal is to reduce what enters and remove what settles without simply sending it back into the air.</p>

  <h2>Separate pollen, dust, smoke and mould</h2>
  <p>Pollen is seasonal material released by plants and carried on air and surfaces. Outdoor dust may come from dry soil, roads, construction or wind. Household dust also develops indoors from fibres, hair and everyday activity, so closing one window will not remove every source.</p>
  <p>Smoke contains much finer particles and gases, while mould is growth associated with moisture. A musty patch or recurring condensation belongs in the <a href="/guides/condensation-and-mould-during-winter/">moisture and mould checks</a>, not a general dusting routine.</p>

  <h2>Find the route that makes dust return</h2>
  <p>Clean one test area, then inspect it over several days. Compare an open-window sill with one that stays closed. Check the floor inside the busiest door and where outdoor clothes or bags are placed.</p>
  <p>A trail from the doorway suggests footwear, paws or traffic. An even layer across a closed room may point to indoor sources, leaks or dust stirred during cleaning.</p>

  <h2>A practical priority order</h2>
  <ol class="feature-list">
    <li>Identify the room and surface where material returns fastest.</li>
    <li>Check its nearest door, window, screen, vent and daily traffic route.</li>
    <li>Use a suitable mat and a workable shoes-off point at the entrance people actually use.</li>
    <li>Reset outdoor clothes, pet paws and carried items before they reach bedrooms or sofas.</li>
    <li>Clean tracks, sills and higher surfaces before the floor.</li>
    <li>Vacuum or collect settled material using tools suited to the surface.</li>
    <li>Consider filtration only if a defined airborne problem remains.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For a whole-home job, use the <a href="/guides/spring-cleaning-checklist/">Spring Cleaning Checklist</a>. If one entry or window is enough for today, the <a href="/guides/20-minute-spring-reset/">20-Minute Spring Reset</a> keeps the task contained.</p>

  <h2>Slow material down at the entrance</h2>
  <p>Place a stable, cleanable mat where there is enough clearance for the door and a safe walking surface. A mat only helps when feet cross it and it is cleaned before accumulated grit is tracked onwards. Put the shoes-off point close enough to be used without blocking the threshold.</p>
  <p>Frequently used doors matter more than a perfect setup at a formal entrance. Avoid leaving one door ajar during repeated trips. Repair obvious seals or screen gaps without blocking required ventilation, drainage or weep holes.</p>

  <h2>Clean windows where material collects</h2>
  <p>Remove loose track debris with a narrow vacuum tool or soft brush where suitable, then wipe the sill, frame and accessible screen frame safely for the finish. A well-wrung damp cloth can collect fine residue on moisture-tolerant hard surfaces, but not every timber finish or delicate screen.</p>
  <p>Do not stretch the mesh. If dust returns around a closed window, inspect the screen fit, seals and frame.</p>

  <h2>Reset washing, bedding and pet traffic</h2>
  <p>National Asthma Council and healthdirect guidance advises avoiding outdoor clothesline drying on high-pollen days for people affected by pollen. Use suitable indoor drying when needed. Keep washing exposed during heavy pollen off beds, and rewash it when the household's health advice requires. Change outdoor clothes before entering bedrooms.</p>
  <p>Wash bedding and covers according to their care labels, and clean soft furnishings appropriately. Do not assume every curtain or cover tolerates hot washing.</p>
  <p>For pets, keep a clean towel near the usual entry and gently wipe visibly dusty paws or coat when appropriate. Never use household cleaning chemicals on an animal. The <a href="/guides/pet-cleaning-supplies-what-to-check-before-buying-cleaning-gear/">pet cleaning guide</a> covers realistic routines.</p>

  <h2>Remove settled dust instead of moving it around</h2>
  <p>Work from higher accessible surfaces towards the floor so fallen material is collected last. A dry feather duster or fast sweeping can redistribute fine material. A suitable vacuum attachment, electrostatic cloth or lightly damp cloth can collect it more deliberately, depending on the surface.</p>
  <p>For a vacuum, floor-head design, exhaust filtration, seals and careful bin or bag handling matter alongside suction. The <a href="/guides/cordless-stick-vacuums-australia/">cordless vacuum guide</a> explains why headline power is not a pickup result, while the <a href="/guides/robot-vacuum-buying-guide-australia/">robot vacuum guide</a> covers regular floor maintenance and the areas robots cannot reach.</p>

  <h2>Ventilate when the outdoor air is useful</h2>
  <p>Do not keep a home permanently closed. Ventilation removes indoor pollutants and moisture, but an open window also connects the room to current outdoor conditions. On heavy pollen, smoke or wind-blown dust days, temporarily close the most exposed openings and ventilate when the outside air improves.</p>
  <p>Keep kitchen, bathroom and laundry exhaust working as intended. Recirculating air conditioning is not fresh-air ventilation; clean owner-accessible filters according to the exact appliance instructions.</p>

  <h2>Know the filtration terms before buying</h2>
  <p>For vacuums, look for a clearly specified high-efficiency exhaust filter and a well-sealed path rather than vague terms such as “HEPA-like”. For a portable air purifier, HEPA describes particle filtration, while Clean Air Delivery Rate (CADR) describes how quickly filtered air is delivered. Room area, ceiling height, usable fan speed and placement all affect the fit.</p>
  <p>Also check noise, power use, filter price, replacement availability and the space required around the air intake and outlet. A purifier sized for one closed bedroom is not automatically a whole-home solution.</p>

  <h2>What an air purifier cannot fix</h2>
  <p>An air purifier can filter some airborne particles. It does not remove settled dust, stop tracked-in material, clean a pet, seal an entry gap or replace necessary ventilation. Performance also falls when it is undersized, obstructed or fitted with an overdue filter.</p>
  <p>Shopping should follow the diagnosis. If entry control and ordinary cleaning keep the problem manageable, another appliance may add cost, noise and filter waste without solving a new problem.</p>

  <h2>Know when cleaning is not the answer</h2>
  <p>This guide does not diagnose allergy, asthma or another condition. If symptoms are persistent, affect sleep or daily activities, or involve breathing changes, discuss them with a doctor or pharmacist rather than escalating household purchases. Building dust from renovation, suspected asbestos, unexplained odour or widespread mould also needs source-specific professional advice.</p>
  <p>Control the busiest entry, reset what comes in from outdoors and collect settled material in a sensible order. Those steps remain useful whether or not filtration later earns a supporting role.</p>

